# Everything here is optional — the app works with this file absent.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ matters: tiles, routing and search are fetched by the BROWSER,
# so these are inlined at build time. A server-only variable would arrive as
# undefined and silently fall back to the default.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the browser asks for the map — THIS SERVER, by default
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tiles, routing, search and Overpass all go to /api/osm/* on this server, and
# the server fetches them: straight out where that works, and through the proxy
# chain below where it does not. Nothing needs setting for that — it is what
# happens with this file absent.
#
# Why it is the default: this app answers from more than one machine, and they
# do not all have the same access. The same build is deployed to both, and a
# visitor may be on a network that reaches neither. Asking THIS server is the
# only arrangement that works in every combination, because the server is the
# one host that is certainly reachable — the page came from it.
#
# This app's OWN API (/api/assess, /api/reports) is never involved. Those are
# same-origin calls to the box that served the page; there is nothing to reach
# around, and /api/osm/ cannot be pointed at them.

# Send the browser straight to OpenStreetMap again, the old way. Fine when
# every visitor and both servers can reach it, and it takes this server out of
# the path for tiles.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OSM_DIRECT=1

# A named upstream beats both of the above, per service. This is the right
# answer for a real deployment, and it removes the rate limit and the
# reachability problem together.
#
# A different tile server, or one you host. OpenStreetMap's tile policy asks
# heavy users to run their own.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TILE_URL=https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
#
# Your own OSRM. The public demo server is rate limited, sometimes down, and
# reliably serves only the driving profile:
#   docker run -t -i -p 5000:5000 -v "${PWD}:/data" osrm/osrm-backend \
#     osrm-routed --algorithm mld /data/netherlands-latest.osrm
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OSRM_URL=http://localhost:5000
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_NOMINATIM_URL=https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OVERPASS_URL=https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter

# Which upstreams the FORWARDER goes to. Server-side, comma-separated; the
# first that answers wins and a retry moves to the next.
# OSM_UPSTREAM_OVERPASS=https://overpass-api.de/api/interpreter,https://overpass.kumi.systems/api/interpreter
# OSM_UPSTREAM_OSRM=https://router.project-osrm.org
# OSM_UPSTREAM_NOMINATIM=https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org
# OSM_UPSTREAM_TILE=https://tile.openstreetmap.org

# ── Getting out when this server's own internet is filtered ──────────────────
# The forwarder tries to fetch OpenStreetMap DIRECTLY first, and only when that
# has been shown to work — it is probed on a schedule, so the machine that
# cannot get out never pays a timeout to rediscover that. When direct does not
# work, it goes through the proxies below.
#
#     browser → this server → a proxy from the list → OpenStreetMap
#
# The list ships in ./proxies.json with one proxy that works from every network
# this is deployed on. Override it inline — comma-separated, or JSON — to use
# your own:
# OSM_PROXY_LIST=176.111.37.5:39811
# OSM_PROXY_LIST=176.111.37.5:39811,203.0.113.9:8080
# OSM_PROXY_LIST_FILE=./proxies.json
#
# With credentials: user:pass@host:port
# OSM_PROXY_LIST=user:pass@176.111.37.5:39811

# ── A second hop in front of the list. RARELY NEEDED ─────────────────────────
# Only for a network where the proxies in the list are themselves reachable
# solely through one other proxy:
#
#     browser → this server → OSM_PROXY_ENTRY → one of OSM_PROXY_LIST → OSM
#
# Leave it unset unless that is your situation. An entry proxy that stops
# answering used to take the whole list down with it; it no longer does — the
# entry is probed on its own, is never blamed on an exit, and is stepped over
# so the list is tried directly — but it is still one more thing to be down.
# OSM_PROXY_ENTRY=

# How long a probe and a real request may take, and how many exits one request
# may try before giving up.
# OSM_PROXY_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=9000
# OSM_PROXY_ENTRY_TIMEOUT_MS=4000
# OSM_PROXY_TIMEOUT_MS=25000
# OSM_PROXY_ATTEMPTS=4
# A ceiling on ONE request across all its attempts, so a dead chain fails the
# page instead of hanging it.
# OSM_PROXY_BUDGET_MS=45000
# How often the list is re-probed in the background.
# OSM_PROXY_PROBE_INTERVAL_MS=600000
# What a probe fetches — an upstream this app actually depends on.
# OSM_PROXY_PROBE_URL=https://overpass-api.de/api/status
# Last-resort direct attempt after every proxy has failed.
# OSM_PROXY_ALLOW_DIRECT=1

# ── The model. SERVER-SIDE, and these are NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_ ───────────────────
# The model writes the sentence. It never produces the score — that is computed
# from the map counts in src/lib/score.ts, where it can be checked. A small
# local model asked to invent a safety number would produce a confident one
# with nothing behind it.
#
# LOCAL FIRST: free per call, private (this app is told where someone is
# walking and at what hour), and it cannot be rate-limited or cut off for a
# billing failure. Unreachable from a phone, which is why this runs on the
# server rather than in the browser.
LOCAL_AI_HOST=192.168.11.165
# Blank uses http://$LOCAL_AI_HOST:1234/v1 — LM Studio's default. The literal
# value `off` disables the local tier and goes straight to the fallback.
LOCAL_AI_URL=
LOCAL_AI_MODEL=gemma-3-4b-it

# ── Liara: the fallback, for when the LAN box is off ─────────────────────────
# BOTH must be set or the tier is skipped entirely. A URL with no key 401s on
# every call, which turns "the LAN box is off" into a confusing error instead
# of a quiet degradation.
# Get these from the Liara console → AI service. Verify before relying on it:
#   curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $LIARA_AI_KEY" "$LIARA_AI_URL/models"
LIARA_AI_URL=
LIARA_AI_KEY=
LIARA_AI_MODEL=

# If neither tier answers, the page still shows the score and the findings.
# Only the sentence is lost.
